<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Second pregnancy at 9 weeks by a certain last menstrual period, with the uterus about 12 to 13 weeks in size and heavier nausea than in her first pregnancy. Please perform a first-trimester ultrasound at 11 weeks to establish the dates and the cause of the large uterus, and advise on her prenatal care. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks; sooner for bleeding, pain, or severe vomiting. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What explains the large uterus: a dating error, a multiple pregnancy, fibroids, or a molar pregnancy?</item><item>If there is more than one fetus, what is the chorionicity, and what surveillance will she need?</item><item>Which aneuploidy screening options suit her pregnancy?</item><item>Does she need low-dose aspirin?</item><item>How should her iron deficiency anemia be managed?</item><item>What should be done to assess and lower the risk of preterm birth?</item><item>When and where should she plan to give birth?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Cleopatra is a 29-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, whose son was born seven years ago after an uncomplicated pregnancy, by her account. Her periods are regular every 28 days and she is certain of her last period, which gives 9 weeks 0 days today. On bimanual examination the uterus is smooth and about 12 to 13 weeks in size. She has nausea most of the day with vomiting two or three times a day, but keeps fluids down (PUQE 9) and has not lost weight; urine ketones negative. No bleeding or pain. Blood pressure 108/66 mmHg; height 165 cm, weight 58.0 kg. Quantitative hCG 152,000 mIU/mL, within the wide normal range for 9 weeks. Hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL, MCV 79 fL, ferritin 14 ng/mL; she starts ferrous sulfate every other day today, with a prenatal vitamin and doxylamine with pyridoxine at bedtime. Other first-visit tests are pending. She had the influenza vaccine today. EPDS 6. No medical history, medicines, or allergies otherwise. She wishes to consider aneuploidy screening [S]</paragraph><list><item>At nine weeks her belly already looks bigger than it did at the same stage with her son, and the nausea is worse; she wants to know why. [S]</item><item>She wants to keep working as long as it is safe. [S]</item><item>Her partner is often away, and she wants a plan for appointments and the birth that she can manage with her household. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Cleopatra VII Philopator was born in early 69 BC (some sources allow late 70 BC), daughter of Ptolemy XII Auletes; her mother is not identified with certainty. [D]</item><item>She was of Macedonian Greek descent, from the dynasty founded by Ptolemy I Soter; her first language was Koine Greek, and she is the only Ptolemaic ruler known to have learned the Egyptian language. [D]</item><item>Plutarch writes that her tongue, &apos;like an instrument of many strings&apos;, could turn to whatever language she pleased, and that she seldom needed an interpreter with Ethiopians, Troglodytes, Hebrews, Arabians, Syrians, Medes, or Parthians. [D]</item><item>She was queen of Egypt from 51 BC to 30 BC. [D]</item><item>Her first child, a son, Ptolemy Caesar, called Caesarion, was born in 47 BC, possibly on 23 June; Plutarch says he &apos;was believed to be&apos; the son of the Roman dictator. [D]</item><item>Mark Antony summoned her to Cilicia in 41 BC; she drew him to Alexandria, where he spent the winter of 41 to 40 BC with her, and he left Egypt by the spring of 40 BC. [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

